+/*
+ * The Mi 8's (SDM845) remote processors -- modem (MPSS), audio DSP, sensors, compute -- and the shared memory they talk
+ * through: the common ground under the Wi-Fi (the WCN3990's firmware is loaded and served by the modem, docs/wifi-plan.md)
+ * and, later, the sound. Nothing here is touched unless the boot-arg mi8rp_step=N asks for it, and a step only does what
+ * its line below says.
+ *
+ *   1  reads   SMEM (dt: qcom,smem@86000000, 2 MB): the table of contents at its end, the partitions between us and each
+ *              remote processor, their items, the SMP2P and GLINK items of the modem and the audio DSP -- what XBL/ABL/UEFI
+ *              left there, and whether any remote processor has run since the phone was started. Writes nothing.
+ *
+ *   2  reads   the SoC's locks for SMEM (dt: qcom,ipc-spinlock-sfpb@1f40000 = Linux's qcom,tcsr-mutex: a lock every 0x1000, SMEM
+ *              uses the fourth): who holds each. This block is touched here for the first time.
+ *   3  writes  takes SMEM's lock the way Linux's qcom_hwspinlock does (write 1, it reads back 1 when ours), and gives it back
+ *              (write 0). Nothing in SMEM changes.
+ *   4  writes  RAM only: our SMP2P item in the partition with the modem (428; theirs is 435) and with the audio DSP (429;
+ *              theirs 443), made as Linux's qcom_smem_alloc_private() makes one, filled in and valid, with the entries Linux
+ *              declares ("master-kernel" ...). No doorbell is rung, no remote processor is started: whoever starts later
+ *              finds the item there, as it would after Linux.
+ *
+ *   5  reads   the firmware in the phone's own "modem" partition (FAT16, 4096-byte sectors; image/): adsp.mdt, mba.mbn, modem.mdt,
+ *              wlanmdsp.mbn, the board data; checks the audio DSP's and the modem's headers against their segment files and
+ *              against the memory the dt reserves for them. Read only, through redwood_ufs_read_named().
+ *
+ *   6  writes  the always-on processor's (AOP, which runs RPMh) mailbox, QMP in its message RAM at 0xc300000: opened as Linux's
+ *              qmp_open() does, then "{class: image, res: load_state, name: adsp, val: on}" and straight after it "... val: off"
+ *              (what qcom_q6v5_pas sends around a start); the doorbell is bit 0 of the APCS register 0x1799000c. Also reads the
+ *              levels RPMh keeps for "cx.lvl". No remote processor is started.
+ *   7  asks    TrustZone to take the audio DSP's header (PAS image 1) and where the image will be (0x8c500000, 30 MB); then
+ *              writes the image's segments from the 'modem' partition into that memory and reads them back. Not started.
+ *   8  starts  the audio DSP: RPMh asked for CX at its top level (Linux's proxy vote; never given back here: what the phone's
+ *              own votes were is not known and a lower one could starve it), the AOP told "on", TrustZone asked to authenticate
+ *              and release it; our SMP2P item answered; watched for mi8rp_wait seconds (default 10) for its "slave-kernel" word
+ *              to say up (bit 1); then asked to stop (our "master-kernel" bit 0, its stop-ack bit 3), shut down by
+ *              TrustZone, the AOP told "off". Refused when its SMP2P item (443) is already there (a start before this one)
+ *              unless mi8rp_again=1.
+ *
+ *   boot-arg mi8rp_mss=N (independent of mi8rp_step), docs/modem-boot-plan.md:
+ *   1  (W2a) reads   the GCC branch clocks the modem subsystem needs (enable and halt words), the RPMh levels of cx/mx/mss, the three
+ *                    AXI halt ports in TCSR. Nothing of the modem subsystem itself is touched.
+ *   2  (W2b) writes  QMP "modem on"; RPMh: cx and mx at their top, mss at its top; the seven GCC clocks on (each waited for, as
+ *                    Linux's clk_branch2); read back; then the clocks it turned on off again, the mss rail given back (position 0),
+ *                    QMP "modem off". The cx and mx votes stay, as in step 8. Still nothing of the modem subsystem is touched.
+ *
+ * Layout and constants are Linux's (drivers/soc/qcom/smem.c: "$TOC" version 1, "$PRT" partitions, entries with canary
+ * 0xa5a5, host 0xfffe = global) and the dt of this phone (MI8-firmware/work/mi8-dtb/36.dts). redwood_adsp.c / redwood_wifi.c
+ * have the same code for the POCO X5 Pro's memory at 0x80900000; the Mi 8's is at 0x86000000.
+ *
+ * Lines go to the loader's log (loader_log_putc) as well as the kernel's: it is the only log that comes back when the kernel
+ * is stopped early (EXP-0137), and it holds 32 KB, so keep them few.
+ */
